        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "What do you do with the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "mango pit",
            "zh": "果核"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " after you eat the fruit? Maybe you just throw it away like most people? But for some creative young Chinese people, the pit isn’t useless at all. They can turn it into a lovely little “pet”. No kidding—this is really happening!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The process of making a mango pit pet is simple and fun. The owner only needs two common tools: a toothbrush and a hairdryer. First, they use the toothbrush to carefully clean the pit under running water, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "removing",
            "ref": "v-remove"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " all the fruit bits. Next, they gently dry its natural “hairs” with the hairdryer until it becomes soft and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "fluffy",
            "zh": "蓬松的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". To make the pit look more like a real pet, some people stick on small “eyes” made of plastic or paper. Others paint it in different colours to give it a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "unique",
            "ref": "v-unique"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " style."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Sounds interesting, doesn’t it? But why do these young people spend time raising such an unusual “pet”? “It brings me great joy and helps me relax,” said one owner. “Mangoes are easy to buy from the market anytime. And unlike a real pet, it doesn’t need food or walks—it’s always there for me when I feel lonely or "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "stressed",
            "ref": "v-stressed"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ".” This cute "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "trend",
            "ref": "v-trend"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " shows how young people find fun and "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "comfort",
            "ref": "v-comfort"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in small, creative things, turning everyday objects into little friends."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
